Document (dev.stxt.website): STXT Language
	Header: @STXT@: Introduction 

	Assert >>
		@STXT@ is a **Human-First** language, where documents 
		are readable and expressive,\\ 
		and at the same time reliable for machines.
		
	Content >>
		> @STXT@ is pronounced "ESS-text" — first the letter `S`, then `text`, like _S-TEXT_.

	Subheader: Documents
	
	Content >>
		@STXT@ is a structured, indentation-based text format aimed at:
		
		* Representing documents and data clearly.
		* Being simple to read and write.
		* Being trivial to parse in any language.
		* Allowing both structured content and free text.
		* Applying content rules via `@stxt.schema` or `@stxt.template`.
	
		An @STXT@ document combines readability with an explicit semantic structure,
		which makes it suitable for documents, wikis, and configurations:
	
	Code >>
		# A simple email, no validation
		Email:
			From: John Smith
			To: Mery Adams
			Cc: Keyla Brown
			Title: Project report
			# The body is a literal text block
			Body Content >>
				Hello Mery!
				The book is finished!

	Subheader: Documents with namespace and schemas
	
	Content >>
		@STXT@ allows the use of namespaces to group documents.
		Schemas or templates can also be defined
		to validate the content of a document.
		
		An `Email` document from the `com.example.docs` namespace:
		
	Code >>
		Email (com.example.docs):
			From: John Smith <john.smith@example.com>
			To: Mery Adams <mery.adams@example.com>
			Cc: Keyla Brown <keyla.brown@example.com>
			Title: Project report
			Body Content >>
				Hello Mery!! 
				The book is finished!!
	Content >>
		The template definition:
		
	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.example.docs
			Structure >>
				Email (com.example.docs):
					From: EMAIL
					To: EMAIL
					Cc: EMAIL
					Bcc: EMAIL
					Title: (?)
					Body Content: (1) TEXT
					Metadata (org.example.meta): (?)